I have no record of it here.
French river, north shore-breakwater, $5,000.	'

In my county in the past there has been a disposition on the part of the people to move, but it is not so now, and that is due not to the fact the hon. gentleman points out, that there are not agents, but to the fact that the wages are rising. I think now of the case of a man who demanded not $30, but $35, a month and his board, and he got it.
Administration of Chinese immigration, salaries, $21,000; contingencies, $13,000.
Mr. MoKENZIE: A document has been placed in my hands which I wish to bring to the attention of the acting Minister of the Interior. It is from a very deserving class of people who come from eastern countries to the province of Nova Scotia, and particularly to the county of Cape Breton. I understand that there is a regulation under which immigrants are compelled to have a certain amount of money when they come into this country -I think it is two hundred dollars-and this blanket regulation, which covers all Asiatics, applies to Syrians coming into Canada. I think I can place before the minister what I have to say in regard to this matter by reading this petition which has been placed in my hands;
The petition of the undersigned Syrian residents of the county of Cape Breton humbly showeth:
That the Syrian population of Nova Scotia is 2,000.
That with the exception of immigrants arriving within the last three years, all the Syrian population are naturalized British subjects;
That in the entire Syrian population there is not one person receiving aid as a pauper;
That the average wealth of the said population is equal to, if it does not exceed, the average wealth of all other nations residing in Nova Scotia;
That the percentage of crime among the said population is smaller than that of any other people in Nova Scotia, and that the entire population are Christians and active church attendants;
Mr. SUTHERLAND.
That the death rate is lower than the average, and that they are entirely free from disease;
That many of the said residents of Nova Scotia are separated from their wives and other members of their families, and that they desire that they should be permitted to immigrate into Canada, particularly in cases where they have homes awaiting them, without being obliged to show that they have two hundred dollars in cash.
Your petitioners humbly submit that it is an injustice to the said population to be placed with Chinese and Japanese in respect to immigration rights, and respectfully submit that they should be admitted into Canada on the same terms as any white European immigrant, and pray that such steps may be taken as will amend the law in this respect.
These Syrians in the county of Cape Breton, two thousand in number, are proving to be first-class citizens. Some of them left their wives and children behind them, and, although they are prepared to look after them, they find it difficult to send $200 to each of them in order to bring them to this country. When a man comes to Canada, becomes a naturalized British subject and establishes himself in a good position, he should be permitted without any restriction to bring his wife and children into this country, provided they are free from disease and are such persons as may reasonably be permitted to enter into Canada.
